WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.160
There's a lot that looks familiar in the&nbsp;
fourth "Matrix" movie, but this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:09.120
Neo has no idea how much things have&nbsp;
changed. If you were blinded by nostalgia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.160
you may have missed these small&nbsp;
details in "The Matrix Resurrections."

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.560
"A déjà vu is usually a glitch in the Matrix."

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.320
During the montage of Tom Anderson's day&nbsp;
to day life, we see him eating noodles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.160
at a restaurant several times. In&nbsp;
a small nod to the series' past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.960
the location directly parallels a line&nbsp;
from the first movie — and provides a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:36.320
maybe subliminal clue to the audience that Neo&nbsp;
has indeed gotten plugged back into the Matrix.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.040
After Neo is sent back into&nbsp;
the Matrix for the first time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.120
he's driven down a familiar street with&nbsp;
Morpheus and Trinity. At one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.440
he points out the car window and identifies&nbsp;
a restaurant he used to like. He then spends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.920
some time pondering how they never actually&nbsp;
happened and how his memories aren't real.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:52.420
"God."

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:53.460
"What?"

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.200
"I used to eat there. Really good noodles."

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:05.360
Fast forward 22 real life years later and who&nbsp;
knows how many in-universe years, and Neo is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.920
back in a simulation again — and dropping hints&nbsp;
about it by eating those really good noodles.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.360
After meeting up with Bugs and the New Morpheus,&nbsp;
Neo is informed that he sees himself differently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.320
than everyone else — literally. He's put in front&nbsp;
of a mirror and shown that, due to technological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.120
shenanigans, most people see him as an old man&nbsp;
with white hair and a white beard. This part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.360
is pretty shocking — provided, of course,&nbsp;
you missed the fact that we saw it already.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.960
"If you want the truth, Neo,&nbsp;
you're going to have to follow me."

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.240
We're definitely given at least&nbsp;
one somewhat obvious hint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.040
when Bugs recalls seeing a man who looked&nbsp;
nothing like Neo but was clearly Neo.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.080
We're also given a much less obvious hint, one&nbsp;
that requires a keen attention to detail. Pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.200
attention to scenes when Tom Anderson is in his&nbsp;
bathroom, especially when he's not looking at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.480
mirror. The Tom everyone else sees — the gray,&nbsp;
haggard man — is shown in the reflection. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:02.000
brief, but it's there, and it's a neat clue for&nbsp;
either observant viewers or those doing a rewatch.&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:02.560
"Where are we?"

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.920
One of the first big revelations of "The&nbsp;
Matrix Resurrections" is that the machines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.440
never returned Neo's body to humanity after he&nbsp;
sacrificed himself. It's certainly an intriguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.440
plot point, but one nobody would bat an eye&nbsp;
at ... unless they were familiar with "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.240
Matrix Online," a massively-multiplayer&nbsp;
online game active in the late aughts.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:26.240
The game was meant as a follow-up to the&nbsp;
original trilogy, with the Wachowskis not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.440
particularly intent on making a fourth movie&nbsp;
at the time. In a 2005 interview with IGN,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.560
the Wachowskis said that they wanted an MMO&nbsp;
because — like their movies — it required&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.560
people to be active participants&nbsp;
rather than passive observers:

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.040
"Those are the people, the people who thought&nbsp;
about it, who worked at it, who we ultimately made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:50.000
the trilogy for and it now makes perfect sense&nbsp;
to us that they should inherit the storyline."

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.320
Sounds like proof that this was&nbsp;
canonical, right? Nope. Ben Chamberlain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.840
the last full-time developer to work on&nbsp;
"The Matrix Online," told Eurogamer in 2021,

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.120
"I don't think we ever worried&nbsp;
about whether our game was canon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.560
I don't think anyone who worked on it ever&nbsp;
thought that anything we were doing would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.080
be regarded as canonical in the&nbsp;
same way that the movies were."

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.120
This is just a way of saying&nbsp;
that the game is not canon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.400
despite massive speculation. The game&nbsp;
infamously saw the assassination of Morpheus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:23.520
but in "Resurrections" we find out that Morpheus&nbsp;
instead lived on as a leader of humanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.000
and died of other causes, leaving a&nbsp;
much-older Niobe to take his place.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.680
But there's one detail that carried over&nbsp;
from "The Matrix Online," and it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:37.280
inciting incident of the game: Neo's body was&nbsp;
never returned by the machines, and interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.920
parties want it back. It's hard to imagine&nbsp;
that Lana Wachowski didn't have that in mind.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.840
"Nothing comforts anxiety&nbsp;
like a little nostalgia."

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:51.840
The first "Matrix" movie introduces&nbsp;
the dangers of deja vu via a black cat.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:52.960
"What did you just say?"

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:58.880
After Neo sees the same cat twice, he's informed&nbsp;
that deja vu is a sign of a glitch in the Matrix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.880
an indicator that something's wrong.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:06.160
It's hard not to get a sense of deja vu in the&nbsp;
fourth movie, which is by design — after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.840
"Resurrections" is both textually and&nbsp;
subtextually about making another "Matrix" movie.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.040
As such, there's at least&nbsp;
one overt reference to this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.760
The Analyst's black cat named Deja&nbsp;
Vu, who Tom Anderson does not like.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.200
But there's one more instance&nbsp;
of deja vu that's easy to miss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.280
unless you're paying close attention.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.800
During the montage showing Tom's day-to-day life,&nbsp;
we see a cluster of birds flying through the sky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:37.120
multiple times. They fly forward before swooping&nbsp;
backwards. What's easy to miss is that the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:43.440
same birds fly in the exact same loop outside the&nbsp;
exact same window — they're a glitch. It's not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.840
coincidence they show up around the same time&nbsp;
as Bugs and Morpheus, and it's certainly not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:53.360
coincidence that we watch a similar group of birds&nbsp;
dissolve mid-swoop towards the end of the movie.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.320
"I remember this."

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.440
It was obvious from the beginning&nbsp;
that the Analyst was keeping Neo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:04.400
placated as Tom Anderson. After all,&nbsp;
he was — in perhaps the least subtle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.840
part of "Resurrections" — the&nbsp;
one prescribing the blue pills.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.160
There's also another striking detail&nbsp;
about him: the blue frames of his glasses.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.800
We don't find out that the Analyst is the one&nbsp;
behind the new Matrix until most of the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:22.320
through "Resurrections," at which point he removes&nbsp;
his glasses for most of the rest of the movie.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:28.160
His glasses act as his personal blue pill in a way&nbsp;
— he gets to see the artificial world he built.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:32.640
When he needs to get aggressive, he removes&nbsp;
his glasses, as if to demonstrate that he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.920
not hiding anymore. We do see him wearing the&nbsp;
blue glasses one more time — at the very end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:43.120
after Neo and Trinity break into his residence&nbsp;
and reveal that they've taken over the Matrix.

